  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "zvNhhp3Cx7d0xE59zWP+l9VWUb4=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:12.667Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Seidor is recorded at EUR 1.121bn of 2024 revenue, up 30%, with about 10,000 staff, and is described as the largest Spanish SAP house alongside a Microsoft practice. The screen's verdict turns on none of those numbers: it is package-led rather than bespoke.\n\nGrowth at that rate normally reads as a positive signal, and here it works against the lead. Fast growth in a package practice means the machine that is working is configuration and rollout of a proprietary suite, so the firm has every reason to keep selling exactly that. Bespoke, client-owned systems are a different delivery motion with different margins, different staffing and a different sales conversation.\n\nThe deeper problem is that SAP already owns the system of record in those engagements. A layer whose value is holding the append-only, user-attributed record of decisions has to be positioned either beside or against the suite the practice exists to implement \u2014 a conversation a EUR 1.1bn SAP partner has no commercial reason to open.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Seidor (ES)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> EUR 1.121bn 2024 (+30%), 10,000 staff. Package-led rather than bespoke.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:12.667390Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:12.667394Z"
    }
  },
